        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://coxsackie.org/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from a Native American word meaning “owl’s hoot,” a phrase still used by locals.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coxsackie%2C_New_York",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Coxsackie was incorporated as a town in 1788, just a decade after the American Revolution ended.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://upstater.com/a-guide-to-coxsackie-ny/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Hudson River borders Coxsackie’s eastern edge, giving the village a scenic waterfront that once powered early industry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coxsackie%2C_New_York",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1974, the Forestville Commonwealth Owenite community at Earlton was added to the National Register of Historic Places.",
